pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Drive a speech engine through its queue: start it, say a text, change rate, volume and voice, and explain why nothing is spoken until the queue is run"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can drive a speech engine through its queue: start it, say a text, change rate, volume and voice, and explain why nothing is spoken until the queue is run, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter24.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Work out the length and the format of speech audio, read and write a WAV file with the standard library, and say which engines save something other than WAV"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can work out the length and the format of speech audio, read and write a WAV file with the standard library, and say which engines save something other than WAV, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter24.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Choose a Whisper model by name and size, list what must be installed for it to run, and read the parts of the dictionary that transcribe returns"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can choose a Whisper model by name and size, list what must be installed for it to run, and read the parts of the dictionary that transcribe returns, at the understand level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter24.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Write the same timed segments as SRT, VTT and TSV, and predict the time stamp, header and file name each format and writer produces"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can write the same timed segments as SRT, VTT and TSV, and predict the time stamp, header and file name each format and writer produces, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter24.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Explain how yt-dlp options, output templates and file names behave, and design glue code whose download, transcription and speech steps can be replaced by stand-ins and tested offline"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can explain how yt-dlp options, output templates and file names behave, and design glue code whose download, transcription and speech steps can be replaced by stand-ins and tested offline, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter24.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "After engine.say('Hello') and before engine.runAndWait(), what has a pyttsx3 engine done?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "say() does not speak; with the dummy driver the list of events was still empty after it." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "No error is raised; the text simply waits in the queue." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It has spoken the text and is waiting for the next line" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It has only queued the text; nothing is spoken yet" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It has raised an error because the queue is not running" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "In pyttsx3 2.99, what does engine.say('') do?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Nothing is queued for an empty text." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "It returns the message Argument value can't be None or empty and does not raise." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It queues a moment of silence" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises ValueError" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It queues nothing and returns an error message" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "At the default rate of 200 words per minute, how long does a text of 1,800 words take to speak?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "0.3 is the seconds per word, not the total." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "1,800 / 200 is 9.0; 360 would come from multiplying." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "9.0 minutes" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0.3 seconds" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "360.0 minutes" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which statement about saving speech with pyttsx3 is correct?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The chapter says WAV only, but the source of the macOS driver says that Apple writes .aiff, not .wav." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The package cannot save MP3 at all; the file name does not choose the format." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Every engine writes only WAV, whatever the file name says" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The macOS driver writes .aiff, while the eSpeak and SAPI5 drivers write WAV" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It writes an MP3 when the file name ends in .mp3" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Under Python 3.14, which of these modules can still be imported?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Remember ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "aifc was removed in Python 3.13." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "audioop was removed in Python 3.13." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "aifc" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "audioop" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "wave" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which Whisper model name is accepted by release 20250625 but is not in the chapter's list?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "There is no model named huge." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "There is no model named base-v3." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "turbo" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "huge" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "base-v3" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Besides the Python packages, what must be installed for Whisper to decode an audio file?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Whisper reads a file; it does not play or record sound." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "eSpeak is for pyttsx3's speech, not for Whisper." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A sound card driver" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The ffmpeg program" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The eSpeak engine" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A segment starts at 5.64 seconds. Which is its start time in Whisper's SRT file?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is the VTT form, without hours and with a full stop." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is the TSV form, in milliseconds." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "00:00:05,640" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "00:05.640" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "5640" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which line is the VTT form of the SRT time 00:00:05,640?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "VTT uses a full stop before the milliseconds, not a comma." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The milliseconds need three digits." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "00:05,640" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "00:05.640" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "00:00:05.64" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Why does the chapter find the downloaded file with Path().glob('downloaded_content.*')?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "glob only finds files that already exist." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "outtmpl can hold a file name; only the extension is left to the format." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The extension depends on the audio format that was downloaded" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "glob is what downloads the file" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The outtmpl option cannot contain a file name" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "yt-dlp is given the title 'What: is? this/that'. What happens to the colon, question mark and slash in the file name?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The chapter says they are excluded, but yt-dlp 2026.8.19 replaces them." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The download is not refused because of a title." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "They are replaced by look-alike characters" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "They are deleted" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The download stops with an error" .
pg:Q12 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Why give speak() the engine as an argument instead of creating it inside?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q12_O0, pg:Q12_O1, pg:Q12_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q12_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Nothing forbids it; the choice is about testing." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "say() returns nothing useful; it only queues." .
pg:Q12_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "pyttsx3 does not allow a global engine" .
pg:Q12_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A recording stand-in can then replace the engine, so the function can be tested without a voice" .
pg:Q12_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "say() returns the audio, which must be passed on" .
pg:TextToSpeechEngine a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text to speech engine"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first half of the chapter teaches pyttsx3: initialising the engine, say and runAndWait, the voice, rate and volume properties (Sweigart, 2025), which the pyttsx3 documentation describes on top of the speech engine of the operating system (Bhat, 2026). Its mid-level subjects are EngineSetup, SpeakingText, VoicesAndProperties, RateAndVolume (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_TextToSpeechEngine> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextToSpeechEngine" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextToSpeechEngine .
pg:EngineSetup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Engine setup"@en ;
    skos:definition "Setting up the engine means the Python package, the speech library under it and the pin of Appendix A (Python Package Index, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). Its leaves are Pyttsx3Package, EspeakDependency, DriverAndLibrary, PinnedReleaseTruncates (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_EngineSetup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EngineSetup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextToSpeechEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EngineSetup .
pg:Pyttsx3Package a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pyttsx3 package"@en ;
    skos:definition "PyPI lists pyttsx3 as a text-to-speech library that works offline and the chapter installs it with pip, while Appendix A pins the release the book was tested with (Python Package Index, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). Pyttsx3 2.99 is the newest release and Appendix A pins 2.98; on Linux it requires no other package ([]), because every requirement it lists is for Windows or macOS, and it imports as pyttsx3 (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_Pyttsx3Package> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Pyttsx3Package" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EngineSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Pyttsx3Package .
pg:EspeakDependency a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Espeak dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's setup note tells Linux readers to install eSpeak, and the pyttsx3 documentation says the engine on Linux is eSpeak (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). On Ubuntu 24.04 pyttsx3 2.99 started with no eSpeak package gave RuntimeError: This means you probably do not have eSpeak or eSpeak-ng installed!; with only the package the chapter names (espeak 1.48.15) it gave ValueError: SetVoiceByName failed with unknown return code -1 for voice: gmw/en; with only espeak-ng it started and listed 131 voices (ok); with both it started (ok) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_EspeakDependency> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EspeakDependency" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EngineSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EspeakDependency .
pg:DriverAndLibrary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Driver and library"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 driver source for Linux loads the eSpeak library through ctypes, and the eSpeak NG README describes the library the project keeps alive (Bhat, 2026) (eSpeak NG developers, 2026). The driver that pyttsx3 2.99 chose on Linux is EspeakDriver, and the library it loaded is libespeak-ng.so.1.1.51, the shared library of eSpeak NG; its drivers for Windows (sapi5) and macOS (nsss) are separate files (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_DriverAndLibrary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DriverAndLibrary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EngineSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DriverAndLibrary .
pg:PinnedReleaseTruncates a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pinned release truncates"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix A names the pyttsx3 release the chapter was tested with, and the chapter prints the sentence Hello. How are you doing?"@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PinnedReleaseTruncates> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PinnedReleaseTruncates" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EngineSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PinnedReleaseTruncates .
pg:SpeakingText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Speaking text"@en ;
    skos:definition "Speaking text is a queue: say adds, runAndWait plays, and a machine with no sound card still runs (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). Its leaves are SayQueuesText, RunAndWaitLoop, NoSoundCard, EmptyTextRefused (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SpeakingText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SpeakingText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextToSpeechEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SpeakingText .
pg:SayQueuesText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Say queues text"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says say() adds text to the queue and that nothing is spoken until runAndWait() is called, and the pyttsx3 documentation describes the same queue (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). Say() returns None at once and no event has fired (0 so far), while isBusy() already reports True: it only queues the text until runAndWait() runs (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SayQueuesText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SayQueuesText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeakingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SayQueuesText .
pg:RunAndWaitLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Run and wait loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation lists the events started-utterance, started-word and finished-utterance and says runAndWait blocks until the queue is empty (Bhat, 2026) (Bhat, 2026). The chapter's program 24-1 fired its events in this order: [('started-utterance', None), ('finished-utterance', None), ('started-utterance', None), ('finished-utterance', None), ('words', ['Hello', 'How', 'are', 'you', 'doing', 'Yes', 'I', 'feeling', 'fine', 'as', 'well'])]; each say() ended with a finished-utterance, and the words of the two sentences were reported once (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_RunAndWaitLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunAndWaitLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeakingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunAndWaitLoop .
pg:NoSoundCard a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "No sound card"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 README says the speech is played through the speakers, and the eSpeak driver writes to the audio device it finds (Bhat, 2026) (Bhat, 2026). On this machine /dev/snd exists: False, aplay is installed: True, and runAndWait() still returned with exit code 0 after aplay printed an audio open error (True); the program cannot tell that nothing was heard (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_NoSoundCard> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoSoundCard" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeakingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoSoundCard .
pg:EmptyTextRefused a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Empty text refused"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation does not say what an empty text does (Bhat, 2026). Say('') returned the text 'Argument value can't be None or empty' instead of raising, and save_to_file('') raised AssertionError; neither queues an empty text (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_EmptyTextRefused> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EmptyTextRefused" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeakingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EmptyTextRefused .
pg:VoicesAndProperties a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Voices and properties"@en ;
    skos:definition "Voices and properties are read with getProperty and changed with setProperty, and the voice list belongs to the machine (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). Its leaves are VoiceObjects, SetPropertyQueued, PersistentVoice (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_VoicesAndProperties> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-VoicesAndProperties" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextToSpeechEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:VoicesAndProperties .
pg:VoiceObjects a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Voice objects"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the voices with getProperty('voices') and the pyttsx3 documentation lists the fields id, name, languages, gender and age of a Voice (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). ESpeak NG 1.51 gave 131 Voice objects, the first being ('Afrikaans', 'Male', None, ['af'], 'gmw/af'); the genders seen were ['Male'] and the ages ['None'], 0 voices had no language, 8 of them were English ones (the first three ids ['gmw/en-029', 'gmw/en', 'gmw/en-gb-scotland']), and the voice the engine starts with is gmw/en; whether any of their names contains David or Zira, the voices the chapter lists on Windows: False (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_VoiceObjects> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-VoiceObjects" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:VoicesAndProperties .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:VoiceObjects .
pg:SetPropertyQueued a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Set property queued"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation says a property set with setProperty takes effect when the queue is processed (Bhat, 2026). In a fresh process the rate is 200, still 200 right after setProperty('rate', 300), and 300 after a runAndWait() that had an utterance to say: setProperty only queues the change (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SetPropertyQueued> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SetPropertyQueued" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:VoicesAndProperties .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SetPropertyQueued .
pg:PersistentVoice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Persistent voice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's listing 4 sets rate, volume and voice in one program, and the pyttsx3 documentation says a property stays until it is changed (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). The chapter's listing 4 sets the rate to 300, the volume to 0.5 and the voice to voices[1].id, which is sem/am here (Amharic), and the same engine object makes the hello.wav of listing 5: it was 0.89 s long, while a fresh engine makes 1.54 s of the same sentence (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PersistentVoice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PersistentVoice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:VoicesAndProperties .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PersistentVoice .
pg:RateAndVolume a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rate and volume"@en ;
    skos:definition "Rate and volume are two numeric properties with documented ranges (Bhat, 2026). Its leaves are RateDuration, VolumeScale, OutOfRangeValues (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_RateAndVolume> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RateAndVolume" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextToSpeechEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RateAndVolume .
pg:RateDuration a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rate duration"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation gives the default rate of 200 words per minute and lets the program change it (Bhat, 2026). The ten-word fox sentence of the chapter lasted 5.44 s at rate 100, 2.7 s at 200 (the default) and 1.76 s at 300 when each setting was made in a fresh process, so the rate is words per minute only roughly (222 measured at 200) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_RateDuration> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RateDuration" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RateAndVolume .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RateDuration .
pg:VolumeScale a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Volume scale"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation gives the volume as a number from 0.0 to 1.0 (Bhat, 2026). The peak sample of the fox sentence was 26394 at volume 1.0, 13024 at 0.5, 2222 at 0.1 and 0 at 0.0, so the volume scales the samples of the WAV file (49 percent at 0.5) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_VolumeScale> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-VolumeScale" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RateAndVolume .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:VolumeScale .
pg:OutOfRangeValues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Out of range values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation gives the range 0.0 to 1.0 for the volume and does not say what a value outside it does (Bhat, 2026). A volume of 5 gave a peak of 32768 (the 16-bit range ends at 32767, so the samples were clipped), against 26394 at 1.0, and a volume of -1 gave 26393, about the same as 1.0; a rate of 'fast' left the length at 2.7 s, as at the default rate: setProperty accepts values it cannot use (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_OutOfRangeValues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OutOfRangeValues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RateAndVolume .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OutOfRangeValues .
pg:SavedSpeechAndRecognition a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Saved speech and recognition"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter saves speech to WAV files with save_to_file and then recognises it with Whisper, whose README and paper describe the models and the transcribe call (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026) (Radford et al., 2022). Its mid-level subjects are SavingWavFiles, WhisperSetup, ModelsAndTable, Transcribing (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SavedSpeechAndRecognition> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SavedSpeechAndRecognition" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SavedSpeechAndRecognition .
pg:SavingWavFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Saving wav files"@en ;
    skos:definition "save_to_file writes the speech of the queue to a file instead of the speakers (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Its leaves are WavFormat, ExtensionIgnored, FirstCallRace, BrokenSaveHangs (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SavingWavFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SavingWavFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavedSpeechAndRecognition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SavingWavFiles .
pg:WavFormat a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Wav format"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls save_to_file with the file name hello.wav and the Python wave module documents how to read such a file (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The file that save_to_file wrote for the chapter's sentence has 1 channel, 2 bytes per sample, 22050 Hz and 33948 frames, which is 1.54 s and 67940 bytes on disk, with a peak sample of 25045; it is written with the wave module of the standard library, and the engine, not the file, sets the format (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WavFormat> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WavFormat" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavingWavFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WavFormat .
pg:ExtensionIgnored a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Extension ignored"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names a WAV file, and the pyttsx3 README does not promise other formats (Sweigart, 2025) (Bhat, 2026). With pyttsx3 2.99 a file named hello.mp3 begins with b'RIFF' and b'WAVE' and ffprobe calls its format wav; hello.aiff is the same ((b'RIFF', b'WAVE')): the eSpeak driver writes a WAV whatever the extension says."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ExtensionIgnored> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExtensionIgnored" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavingWavFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExtensionIgnored .
pg:FirstCallRace a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "First call race"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation describes runAndWait as blocking until the commands in the queue are done (Bhat, 2026). In five fresh processes each, a 33-word text was not yet complete when the first runAndWait() returned in at least one process (True), and a 5-word text was complete in all five (True): the file can still be written after the call that is meant to wait for it, so a program must wait for the file before it opens it (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_FirstCallRace> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FirstCallRace" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavingWavFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FirstCallRace .
pg:BrokenSaveHangs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Broken save hangs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 documentation does not describe what happens when the file cannot be written (Bhat, 2026). Saving into a folder that does not exist as the first call of a process returned (0), but after one finished utterance the same call never returned (NO RETURN after 15 s), and a second runAndWait() with nothing to say after a property change never returned either (NO RETURN); the error is raised inside a callback of the driver and the loop is never ended (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_BrokenSaveHangs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BrokenSaveHangs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavingWavFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BrokenSaveHangs .
pg:WhisperSetup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Whisper setup"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whisper needs a large Python package and the ffmpeg program, and Appendix A pins a release (OpenAI, 2026) (Python Package Index, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026). Its leaves are WhisperPackage, PinnedInstallFails, FfmpegRequired, WhisperReleases (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WhisperSetup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhisperSetup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavedSpeechAndRecognition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhisperSetup .
pg:WhisperPackage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Whisper package"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper README says to install it with pip install -U openai-whisper and that it needs ffmpeg, and PyPI lists the requirements of the package (OpenAI, 2026) (Python Package Index, 2026). Openai-whisper 20250625 (PyPI uploaded 2025-06-26) requires Python >=3.8 and 7 other packages including torch and numba; the PyPI project named whisper is another program (Fixed size round-robin style database), which the chapter warns about (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WhisperPackage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhisperPackage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhisperPackage .
pg:PinnedInstallFails a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pinned install fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix A pins openai-whisper to the release 20231117 and the Whisper README describes the install (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026). Appendix A pins openai-whisper 20231117, whose only file on PyPI is a source archive; pip download of it ended with return code 1 and the error ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pkg_resources' on Python 3.12.3, and it installed only after setuptools below 81 was installed and the build isolation was switched off, giving 20231117 (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PinnedInstallFails> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PinnedInstallFails" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PinnedInstallFails .
pg:FfmpegRequired a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Ffmpeg required"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper README lists ffmpeg as a requirement and the source of audio.py runs the ffmpeg program to read a file (OpenAI, 2026) (OpenAI, 2026). Without ffmpeg on the PATH whisper.load_audio raised FileNotFoundError; with it, a WAV file became a float32 array of 43129 samples at 16000 Hz, whatever the format of the file was, because Whisper starts ffmpeg to read it; Appendix A never mentions ffmpeg (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_FfmpegRequired> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FfmpegRequired" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FfmpegRequired .
pg:WhisperReleases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Whisper releases"@en ;
    skos:definition "PyPI lists the releases of openai-whisper from 2023 to 2025 and the Whisper README describes the command line and the Python use (Python Package Index, 2026) (OpenAI, 2026). PyPI lists 13 releases of openai-whisper, from 2023-01-18 to 20250625 of 2025-06-26; on Python 3.12.3 6 of them ran the same checks, 6 could not be installed because they require triton 2.0.0, which has no wheel for 3.12, and the first (20230117) has no get_writer; the items that differed between the releases that ran were ['available_models', 'languages in the tokenizer', 'the chapter\\'s two lines write_function(result, 'audio')', 'tiny transcribes fox.wav as', 'transcribe has word_timestamps, clip_timestamps, hallucination_silence_threshold, carry_initial_prompt', 'transcribe: number of parameters', 'writer call signature'] (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WhisperReleases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhisperReleases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhisperReleases .
pg:ModelsAndTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Models and table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The models differ in size, speed and memory, and the chapter's Table 24-1 lists them (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026). Its leaves are ModelFiles, ModelTimes, MemoryAndGpuLimits (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ModelsAndTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModelsAndTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavedSpeechAndRecognition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModelsAndTable .
pg:ModelFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Model files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper README lists the sizes tiny, base, small, medium, large and turbo with their memory needs and speeds, and the chapter reproduces Table 24-1 (OpenAI, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). The files of the models are 75572083 bytes for tiny, 145262807 for base, 483617219 for small, 1528008539 for medium and 3087371615 for large-v3, where the chapter's Table 24-1 says 74MB, 142MB, 472MB, 1.5GB and 3GB; the package knows 14 model names, and the name in the URL of each file is the start of its SHA-256 (65147644a518d12f for tiny) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ModelFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModelFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModelsAndTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModelFiles .
pg:ModelTimes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Model times"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 24-1 of the chapter gives runtimes for the models and the Whisper README gives relative speeds (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026). On two CPU cores the 10-word sample took 1.0 s with tiny, 1.6 s with base and 5.1 s with small, where the chapter's table says 1.9, 3.0 and 9.6; the 771.6-s (about 13-minute) sample took 40.0 s, 98.9 s and 197.5 s, where the table says 1m 20s, 2m 34s and 6m 37s for a 15-minute one (single runs) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ModelTimes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModelTimes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModelsAndTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModelTimes .
pg:MemoryAndGpuLimits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Memory and gpu limits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper README gives the memory each model needs and says the GPU is used when it is available, and the PyTorch pages describe the CUDA builds (OpenAI, 2026) (PyTorch Foundation, 2026) (PyTorch Foundation, 2026). On this machine with 8029 MiB of memory, loading medium and turbo ended with return code -9 and return code -9 (the kernel's out-of-memory killer), and large-v3 (a 3087371615-byte file) could not be loaded under a 6.5 GB limit (RuntimeError); device='cuda' raised RuntimeError because torch.cuda.is_available() is False: no GPU was used and none of the larger models was transcribed (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_MemoryAndGpuLimits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MemoryAndGpuLimits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModelsAndTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MemoryAndGpuLimits .
pg:Transcribing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Transcribing"@en ;
    skos:definition "transcribe returns a dictionary with the text, the segments and the language (OpenAI, 2026) (OpenAI, 2026). Its leaves are ResultStructure, TextVersusBook, LanguagesAndDetection (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_Transcribing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Transcribing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SavedSpeechAndRecognition .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Transcribing .
pg:ResultStructure a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Result structure"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper source of transcribe.py builds a dict with text, segments and language (OpenAI, 2026). Transcribe returns a dict with the keys ['language', 'segments', 'text']; each of its 1 segment has the keys ['avg_logprob', 'compression_ratio', 'end', 'id', 'no_speech_prob', 'seek', 'start', 'temperature', 'text', 'tokens'], and the language is en; the chapter uses only result['text'] and, in the subtitle listings, result['segments'] with start, end and text (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ResultStructure> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResultStructure" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Transcribing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResultStructure .
pg:TextVersusBook a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text versus book"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the transcript of its spoken sentence in a listing, and the transcribe.py source shows that the text is the join of the segments (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026). Whisper tiny wrote  Hello, how are you doing?"@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_TextVersusBook> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextVersusBook" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Transcribing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextVersusBook .
pg:LanguagesAndDetection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Languages and detection"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper paper describes a model trained for many languages with language identification, and the README lists the languages (Radford et al., 2022) (OpenAI, 2026). Whisper knows 100 languages and raised ValueError for 'Klingon'; given eSpeak NG speech in six languages, tiny named the language right 3 times out of 6 and base 4 times, hearing the Turkish sentence as Thai both times, so the language should be given when it is known (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_LanguagesAndDetection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LanguagesAndDetection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Transcribing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LanguagesAndDetection .
pg:SubtitleFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Subtitle files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes subtitle files with the writers of whisper.utils in the formats SRT, WebVTT and TSV, which SubRip and the W3C specification define (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026) (Wikipedia, 2026) (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026). Its mid-level subjects are SubtitleFormats, WhisperWriters, ConvertingFormats, SpeechAccuracy (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SubtitleFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SubtitleFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SubtitleFiles .
pg:SubtitleFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Subtitle formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "SRT, WebVTT and TSV are three plain-text formats for timed text (Wikipedia, 2026) (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026) (OpenAI, 2026). Its leaves are SrtListing, VttListing, TsvListing (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SubtitleFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SubtitleFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SubtitleFormats .
pg:SrtListing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Srt listing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The SubRip article describes blocks of a number, a time range and text, and the chapter's listing 7 prints such a file (Wikipedia, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). Whisper's SRT writer wrote 223 bytes for the two segments the book prints, as blocks of a number, a time range with a comma before the milliseconds and the text, starting ['1', '00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,640']; the file equals the chapter's listing 7 (True), also under the pinned release (True) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SrtListing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SrtListing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFormats .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SrtListing .
pg:VttListing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Vtt listing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The WebVTT specification of the W3C defines the header WEBVTT and a full stop before the milliseconds, and the chapter's listing 8 prints a file (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). The WebVTT writer wrote 215 bytes that begin with WEBVTT, use a full stop before the milliseconds, leave the hours out under an hour (00:00.000 --> 00:05.640) and carry no block numbers; the file equals the chapter's listing 8 (True) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_VttListing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-VttListing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFormats .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:VttListing .
pg:TsvListing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tsv listing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source of utils.py in Whisper defines a writer for tab-separated values with start and end in milliseconds, and the chapter's listing 9 prints one (OpenAI, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). The TSV writer wrote 190 bytes with 6 tab characters and start and end times in whole milliseconds (['0', '5640']); the chapter's listing 9 separates the columns with spaces, so it is not what the writer writes (matches the book: False) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_TsvListing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TsvListing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFormats .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TsvListing .
pg:WhisperWriters a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Whisper writers"@en ;
    skos:definition "The writers of whisper.utils turn a result into a file in one call (OpenAI, 2026). Its leaves are GetWriter, WriterOptions, TimestampGrid (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WhisperWriters> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhisperWriters" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhisperWriters .
pg:GetWriter a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Get writer"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source of utils.py defines get_writer and the writer classes for txt, vtt, srt, tsv, json and all (OpenAI, 2026). Get_writer('all', folder) wrote ['hello.json', 'hello.srt', 'hello.tsv', 'hello.txt', 'hello.vtt'] for the audio name talks/hello.wav (the folder and the extension of the name are dropped), the name 'docx' raised KeyError, and a folder that does not exist raised FileNotFoundError; the writer classes are ['WriteJSON', 'WriteSRT', 'WriteTSV', 'WriteTXT', 'WriteVTT'] (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_GetWriter> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GetWriter" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperWriters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GetWriter .
pg:WriterOptions a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Writer options"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source of utils.py gives the SRT and VTT writers the options max_line_width, max_line_count, max_words_per_line and highlight_words (OpenAI, 2026). On a human reading of the Gettysburg Address the SRT writer made 26 blocks, and 31 blocks with max_line_width=30 and max_line_count=2; with word_timestamps=True the first segment held 14 words, each with its own start and end: the chapter shows none of these options (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WriterOptions> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WriterOptions" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperWriters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WriterOptions .
pg:TimestampGrid a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Timestamp grid"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper paper says timestamps are predicted as tokens at a fixed resolution, and the transcribe.py source rounds them accordingly (Radford et al., 2022) (OpenAI, 2026). The start and end of 52 of 52 segment times in the subtitles of a human reading were multiples of 0.02 s, the 20 ms step of the model's time tokens, so a subtitle time is never finer than that (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_TimestampGrid> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TimestampGrid" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhisperWriters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TimestampGrid .
pg:ConvertingFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Converting formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "Converting between formats is a matter of the header, the separator of the milliseconds and the units (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026) (Wikipedia, 2026). Its leaves are SrtToVtt, TimeFormats, ReadBackCues (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ConvertingFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConvertingFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConvertingFormats .
pg:SrtToVtt a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Srt to vtt"@en ;
    skos:definition "The W3C specification and the SubRip article together define the two formats, so a conversion needs the header, the full stop and the removal of the numbers (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026) (Wikipedia, 2026). A converter of 8 lines, written for this study, turned Whisper's SRT text into text equal to the WebVTT writer's output (True): it drops the block numbers, changes the comma of the times to a full stop, cuts the hours that are zero and adds the WEBVTT header (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SrtToVtt> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SrtToVtt" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConvertingFormats .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SrtToVtt .
pg:TimeFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Time formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "The W3C specification writes the hours as optional in WebVTT and SRT always writes them (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026) (Wikipedia, 2026). The same instant 3601.5 s is written 01:00:01,500 in SRT and 01:00:01.500 in WebVTT, 3725.25 s with the hours forced is 01:02:05,250, and 59.9996 s is rounded up to 01:00.000 (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_TimeFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TimeFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConvertingFormats .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TimeFormats .
pg:ReadBackCues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Read back cues"@en ;
    skos:definition "The TSV writer in utils.py writes plain text with tabs between the columns, so any program that splits lines on tabs can read it back (OpenAI, 2026). Read back from the TSV file, the cues start at 0.0 s and 5.64 s and end at 14.96 s; the start and end are milliseconds as text (5640) that must be divided by 1000, and csv with its default comma finds 1 column (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ReadBackCues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReadBackCues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConvertingFormats .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReadBackCues .
pg:SpeechAccuracy a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Speech accuracy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Accuracy is measured with the word error rate against a reference text (Wikipedia, 2026) (Radford et al., 2022). Its leaves are ModelWerTable, HumanReadings, SilenceHallucination (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SpeechAccuracy> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SpeechAccuracy" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubtitleFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SpeechAccuracy .
pg:ModelWerTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Model wer table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Wikipedia article on word error rate defines it as the sum of substitutions, deletions and insertions divided by the words of the reference, and the Whisper paper reports it for the models (Wikipedia, 2026) (Radford et al., 2022). The word error rate on the ten-word sentence was 0.3 for tiny, 0.2 for base and 0.1 for small, and on the 1,800 words of Alice's Adventures in Wonderland read by eSpeak NG it was 0.1919, 0.1079 and 0.0345; the larger the model, the lower the error, as the chapter's table implies (single runs, one voice, one text) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ModelWerTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModelWerTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeechAccuracy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModelWerTable .
pg:HumanReadings a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Human readings"@en ;
    skos:definition "LibriVox publishes volunteer recordings in the public domain, and the Gettysburg Address is a public speech (LibriVox, 2026) (LibriVox and the Internet Archive, 2026) (Wikipedia, 2026). On four LibriVox readings of the Gettysburg Address (public domain, 100 to 161 s) the word error rate against the Wikipedia copy of the Bliss text lay between 0.1636 and 0.3123 for tiny, between 0.1524 and 0.2565 for base and between 0.0186 and 0.2416 for small; part of it is the readers' own wording and the spoken LibriVox announcement, so the figures only compare the models (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_HumanReadings> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HumanReadings" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeechAccuracy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HumanReadings .
pg:SilenceHallucination a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Silence hallucination"@en ;
    skos:definition "The model card of whisper-large-v3 on Hugging Face warns that the model can write text that is not in the audio (Hugging Face, 2026), and the Whisper paper describes the training data (Radford et al., 2022). Ten seconds of digital silence were transcribed by tiny as  You in 1 segment, and ten seconds of seeded noise as  in 0 segments: Whisper can write words where nobody spoke, so a transcript is not proof of speech (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SilenceHallucination> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SilenceHallucination" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpeechAccuracy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SilenceHallucination .
pg:DownloadingVideosFromWebsites a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Downloading videos from websites"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses yt-dlp to download audio from a video address and to read the metadata of a video, and the yt-dlp documentation describes both (Sweigart, 2025) (YtDlp developers, 2026); the YouTube Terms of Service limit what may be downloaded (Google, 2026). Its mid-level subjects are YtDlpBasics, MetadataOfVideos, RightsAndEthics, DriftOfDownloaders (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_DownloadingVideosFromWebsites> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DownloadingVideosFromWebsites" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DownloadingVideosFromWebsites .
pg:YtDlpBasics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Yt dlp basics"@en ;
    skos:definition "yt-dlp installs as a package, downloads with download and converts with a postprocessor (Python Package Index, 2026) (YtDlp developers, 2026) (FFmpeg developers, 2026). Its leaves are YtDlpPackage, DownloadCall, ExtractAudioPostprocessor (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_YtDlpBasics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-YtDlpBasics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DownloadingVideosFromWebsites .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:YtDlpBasics .
pg:YtDlpPackage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Yt dlp package"@en ;
    skos:definition "PyPI lists yt-dlp as a fork of youtube-dl, and its README describes the Python embedding used by the chapter (Python Package Index, 2026) (YtDlp developers, 2026). Yt-dlp 2026.8.19 (requires Python >=3.10) is the newest release on PyPI that is not a development build; PyPI lists 641 releases of it, 124 of them in the stable numbering, and its newest upload is the development build 2026.10.10.233141.dev0, which pip does not take unless asked (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_YtDlpPackage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-YtDlpPackage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:YtDlpBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:YtDlpPackage .
pg:DownloadCall a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Download call"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp documentation describes YoutubeDL.download taking a list of addresses and returning a code (YtDlp developers, 2026) (YtDlp developers, 2026). With the address of a public-domain LibriVox reading on the Internet Archive, ydl.download() returned 0 and left the files ['downloaded_content.m4a'], as listing 13 expects; given a string instead of a list it still returned 0, and for an address that is nowhere it raised DownloadError with HTTP Error 404 (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_DownloadCall> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DownloadCall" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:YtDlpBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DownloadCall .
pg:ExtractAudioPostprocessor a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Extract audio postprocessor"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp documentation lists the FFmpegExtractAudio postprocessor with the options preferredcodec and preferredquality, and FFmpeg documents the encoders (YtDlp developers, 2026) (FFmpeg developers, 2026). The 1.53 MiB file that yt-dlp fetched was converted by FFmpegExtractAudio into an aac stream in an m4a container of 100.3 s at 69902 bit/s, while the 64 kbit/s file on the archive is an mp3 of 100.4 s: the m4a is a re-encoding, not the original bytes (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ExtractAudioPostprocessor> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExtractAudioPostprocessor" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:YtDlpBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExtractAudioPostprocessor .
pg:MetadataOfVideos a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Metadata of videos"@en ;
    skos:definition "extract_info returns the metadata of a video as a dictionary (YtDlp developers, 2026) (YtDlp developers, 2026). Its leaves are InfoDictionary, SanitizeInfo, KeysOfBook (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_MetadataOfVideos> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MetadataOfVideos" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DownloadingVideosFromWebsites .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MetadataOfVideos .
pg:InfoDictionary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Info dictionary"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp source documents the info dictionary extract_info returns, and the chapter prints a few keys (YtDlp developers, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). For the video of the chapter, extract_info returned a dictionary of 76 keys with the title 'Beyond the Basic Stuff with Python - Al Sweigart - Part 1', a duration of 565 seconds and 16 formats, and metadata.json written from it had 557422 bytes, with no video downloaded (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_InfoDictionary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InfoDictionary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MetadataOfVideos .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InfoDictionary .
pg:SanitizeInfo a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sanitize info"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp source defines sanitize_info as a static method that makes the dictionary JSON-serialisable (YtDlp developers, 2026). The chapter's prose names ydl.sanitize() but the listing calls ydl.sanitize_info(): the class YoutubeDL has sanitize (False) and sanitize_info (True) in yt-dlp 2026.8.19, and the result of sanitize_info is a plain dictionary that json.dumps can write (dict) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SanitizeInfo> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SanitizeInfo" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MetadataOfVideos .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SanitizeInfo .
pg:KeysOfBook a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Keys of book"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the keys id, title, channel, duration and others of the info dictionary (Sweigart, 2025), and the yt-dlp source documents the fields (YtDlp developers, 2026). Of the keys the chapter prints for the video, 13 of 13 were present in what yt-dlp returned on the day and none was missing (True), and the first 13 keys came in the book's order (True); the listing prints a dict_keys, which is a view and not a list (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_KeysOfBook> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-KeysOfBook" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MetadataOfVideos .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:KeysOfBook .
pg:RightsAndEthics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rights and ethics"@en ;
    skos:definition "What may be downloaded depends on the terms of the site and the licence of the work (Google, 2026) (LibriVox, 2026). Its leaves are YouTubeTerms, PublicDomainSource, NotExecutedYoutube (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_RightsAndEthics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RightsAndEthics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DownloadingVideosFromWebsites .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RightsAndEthics .
pg:YouTubeTerms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "You tube terms"@en ;
    skos:definition "The YouTube Terms of Service forbid accessing, reproducing, downloading or distributing content except as the service allows (Google, 2026). The YouTube Terms of Service, section Permissions and Restrictions, say: You are not allowed to: access, reproduce, download, distribute, transmit, broadcast, display, sell, license, alter, modify or otherwise use any part of the Service or any Content except: (a) as expre (this study fetched the page: True); the chapter says nothing about it, and the study therefore asked YouTube for metadata only and downloaded no video from it (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_YouTubeTerms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-YouTubeTerms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RightsAndEthics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:YouTubeTerms .
pg:PublicDomainSource a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Public domain source"@en ;
    skos:definition "LibriVox places its recordings in the public domain and the Internet Archive hosts them (LibriVox, 2026) (LibriVox and the Internet Archive, 2026), and yt-dlp supports many sites besides YouTube (YtDlp developers, 2026). In place of YouTube the study used 4 LibriVox readings of the Gettysburg Address, volunteer recordings in the public domain hosted by the Internet Archive, of 556 s in total, and the 269-word text of the Wikipedia copy of the Bliss version as the reference for word error rates (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PublicDomainSource> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PublicDomainSource" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RightsAndEthics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PublicDomainSource .
pg:NotExecutedYoutube a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Not executed youtube"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp list of supported sites includes YouTube and the Terms of Service restrict downloading from it (YtDlp developers, 2026) (Google, 2026). Of the 46 statements of the chapter's 17 listings, 3 were not executed in any row of the replay: the two downloads from YouTube (listings 11 and 12) and the lyrics of listing 17, which are text; listing 12 was run again with an archive.org address, and nothing was downloaded from YouTube (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_NotExecutedYoutube> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NotExecutedYoutube" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RightsAndEthics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NotExecutedYoutube .
pg:DriftOfDownloaders a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Drift of downloaders"@en ;
    skos:definition "A downloader depends on sites that change, on Python releases and on ffmpeg (Wikipedia, 2026) (Python Package Index, 2026) (FFmpeg developers, 2026). Its leaves are YtDlpReleases, BotCheck, NoFfmpegDownload (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_DriftOfDownloaders> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DriftOfDownloaders" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DownloadingVideosFromWebsites .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DriftOfDownloaders .
pg:YtDlpReleases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Yt dlp releases"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp project publishes a release almost every few weeks, and Wikipedia describes the history of the project (Python Package Index, 2026) (Wikipedia, 2026). The last stable release of each year from 2021 to 2026 (6 of them) was installed on Python 3.14.6 and asked the same questions; 5 ran, and the one that did not, 2021.12.27, stopped at import with No module named 'imghdr' because it imports imghdr, a module Python 3.13 removed; the items that differed between the rest were ['number of extractors', 'sanitize_info of a dict with a function inside'] (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_YtDlpReleases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-YtDlpReleases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DriftOfDownloaders .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:YtDlpReleases .
pg:BotCheck a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Bot check"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp documentation mentions that sites ask for sign-in or cookies when they suspect automation (YtDlp developers, 2026) (YtDlp developers, 2026). On 2026-10-14 one request for the metadata of the chapter's video failed with 'Sign in to confirm you’re not a bot' and a later one from the same machine succeeded (True): what a downloader can fetch from a website depends on the day, the address and the site's checks (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_BotCheck> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BotCheck" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DriftOfDownloaders .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BotCheck .
pg:NoFfmpegDownload a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "No ffmpeg download"@en ;
    skos:definition "The FFmpeg download page lists how to install the tools and the yt-dlp documentation says the postprocessors need them (FFmpeg developers, 2026) (YtDlp developers, 2026). With no ffmpeg or ffprobe on the PATH the same download raised DownloadError (ffprobe and ffmpeg not found."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_NoFfmpegDownload> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoFfmpegDownload" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DriftOfDownloaders .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoFfmpegDownload .
pg:PracticeProgramsAndAnswers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice programs and answers"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with practice questions, answered in Appendix B, and three practice programs: a guessing game that speaks, the song of 99 bottles and a transcriber of videos (Sweigart, 2025) (Sweigart, 2025). Its mid-level subjects are GuessTheNumberWithVoice, NinetyNineBottles, YoutubeTranscriber, QuestionsAndAnswers (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PracticeProgramsAndAnswers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticeProgramsAndAnswers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticeProgramsAndAnswers .
pg:GuessTheNumberWithVoice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Guess the number with voice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first practice program gives the guessing game a voice (Sweigart, 2025). Its leaves are SpeakFunction, GameSpoken, NumbersSpoken (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_GuessTheNumberWithVoice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GuessTheNumberWithVoice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeProgramsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GuessTheNumberWithVoice .
pg:SpeakFunction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Speak function"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's listing 16 adds speech to the guessing game of chapter 3 and relies on a speak function (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter's listing 16 calls speak(), which no listing defines: replayed as printed it raised NameError: name 'speak' is not defined in the newest row, the pinned row and the row without ffmpeg (True); the practice program asks the reader to write it, and the study's short version of it, which calls say() and runAndWait() or writes a WAV file, made the program of listing 15 speak (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SpeakFunction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SpeakFunction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:GuessTheNumberWithVoice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SpeakFunction .
pg:GameSpoken a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Game spoken"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice program asks for the guessing game to speak its lines instead of printing them (Sweigart, 2025). A guessing game whose speak() wrote WAV files with eSpeak NG said 4 lines in a game of 3 guesses; the four files lasted [2.56, 1.94, 1.95, 2.5] s, and a stand-in that recorded the texts showed what the game said without any sound (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_GameSpoken> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GameSpoken" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:GuessTheNumberWithVoice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GameSpoken .
pg:NumbersSpoken a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Numbers spoken"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eSpeak NG documentation describes reading numbers as words in the language of the voice (eSpeak NG developers, 2026) (Duddington, 2026). The game speaks a number as the text of its f-string: the guess 42 became Your guess, 42, is too low., which eSpeak NG reads as a number; the chapter's check, that a guess is a number the voice can read, holds (True) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_NumbersSpoken> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NumbersSpoken" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:GuessTheNumberWithVoice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NumbersSpoken .
pg:NinetyNineBottles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Ninety nine bottles"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second practice program builds and sings the song of 99 bottles (Sweigart, 2025) (Wikipedia, 2026). Its leaves are SongLyrics, SongAudio, SongTranscribed (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_NinetyNineBottles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NinetyNineBottles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeProgramsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NinetyNineBottles .
pg:SongLyrics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Song lyrics"@en ;
    skos:definition "Wikipedia describes 99 Bottles of Beer as a counting song, and the chapter asks for a program that builds the lyrics (Wikipedia, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025). The lyrics the program builds have 99 verses, 396 lines, 2377 words and 11457 characters, and the last line is 'No more bottles of beer on the wall'; the singular bottle is a special case in two lines of each verse, and the song is spoken, not sung (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SongLyrics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SongLyrics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:NinetyNineBottles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SongLyrics .
pg:SongAudio a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Song audio"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks for the song to be saved as audio, and the wave module reads the result (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The 99 verses became 699.3 s (11.7 minutes) of audio and 30.8 MB of WAV file after 7.5 s of work, while 3 verses took 18.3 s of audio; at 22,050 Hz mono 16-bit a minute of speech is about 2.6 MB, so the size grows with the length, not with the voice (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SongAudio> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SongAudio" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:NinetyNineBottles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SongAudio .
pg:SongTranscribed a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Song transcribed"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Whisper paper measures recognition on read and conversational speech, not on songs, and Daisy Bell is the song a computer first sang (Radford et al., 2022) (Wikipedia, 2026). Whisper tiny heard the 3-verse WAV of the song as text that had a word error rate of 0.9589 (the text: 3, turn live again 2, tu...); the same audio gave other words on other runs, because Whisper samples with a random temperature when it doubts a segment, so a repeated or counted sentence is a poor test of an engine (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SongTranscribed> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SongTranscribed" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:NinetyNineBottles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SongTranscribed .
pg:YoutubeTranscriber a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Youtube transcriber"@en ;
    skos:definition "The third practice program downloads, transcribes and writes subtitles for a list of addresses (Sweigart, 2025). Its leaves are TranscribeLoop, ThreeFormats, FailureStopsList (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_YoutubeTranscriber> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-YoutubeTranscriber" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeProgramsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:YoutubeTranscriber .
pg:TranscribeLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Transcribe loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's practice program downloads each video, extracts the audio and transcribes it in a loop (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter's transcriber loop, run with two archive.org addresses in place of YouTube, took 47.8 s with the tiny model for two readings of 26 and 17 segments, which is more than the transcription alone because every address is downloaded and converted first (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_TranscribeLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TranscribeLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:YoutubeTranscriber .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TranscribeLoop .
pg:ThreeFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Three formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks for an SRT, a VTT and a TXT file for each video, and the writers of Whisper make them (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026). The loop wrote 6 files, an SRT, a VTT and a TXT for each address, and the first SRT block began '00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000'; the first words are the LibriVox announcement, not the speech (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ThreeFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ThreeFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:YoutubeTranscriber .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ThreeFormats .
pg:FailureStopsList a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Failure stops list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The yt-dlp documentation says download raises DownloadError on failure unless errors are ignored (YtDlp developers, 2026). An address that cannot be downloaded raised DownloadError out of the loop and stopped the whole list (True); the chapter's loop has no try statement, so the videos after the bad one are never transcribed (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_FailureStopsList> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FailureStopsList" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:YoutubeTranscriber .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FailureStopsList .
pg:QuestionsAndAnswers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Questions and answers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B answers the questions of the chapter (Sweigart, 2025). Its leaves are AnswersAppendixB, DefaultModelAnswer, OfflineAnswer (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_QuestionsAndAnswers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-QuestionsAndAnswers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeProgramsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:QuestionsAndAnswers .
pg:AnswersAppendixB a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Answers appendix b"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B gives the answers to the practice questions of chapter 24 (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B gives 7 answers to the questions of chapter 24 and says in the fifth that Whisper's default model is 'base', in the third that neither pyttsx3 nor Whisper needs an online service, and in the seventh that yt-dlp can download from hundreds of websites (True) (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_AnswersAppendixB> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AnswersAppendixB" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:QuestionsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AnswersAppendixB .
pg:DefaultModelAnswer a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Default model answer"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B says the default model of the command line is base, and the Whisper README and the source of cli/transcribe.py name the default (Sweigart, 2025) (OpenAI, 2026) (OpenAI, 2026). Load_model has the parameters ['name', 'device', 'download_root', 'in_memory'] and no default for name, so whisper.load_model() raised TypeError; the command-line tool whisper takes 'turbo' as its default model in 20250625 and 'small' in the pinned 20231117, so the 'base' of Appendix B answer 5 is the model of the chapter's listing, not a default of either (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_DefaultModelAnswer> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DefaultModelAnswer" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:QuestionsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DefaultModelAnswer .
pg:OfflineAnswer a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Offline answer"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B says the programs of the chapter can work without internet access for speech and recognition (Sweigart, 2025), and the Whisper README says the models are downloaded once (OpenAI, 2026). With the tiny model already on disk, a program that saved speech and transcribed it ran with exit code 0 inside a network namespace with no interface; with no model file and no route it raised URLError, and with the internet it downloaded 75572083 bytes: the answer is true after the first download (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_OfflineAnswer> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OfflineAnswer" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:QuestionsAndAnswers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OfflineAnswer .
pg:EnvironmentAndEvidence a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Environment and evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "The study ran Python 3.14.6 on Ubuntu 24.04 with eSpeak NG 1.51 and states what it could not run (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Canonical, 2026). Its mid-level subjects are Interpreters, Platforms, Reproducibility, HonestyOfTheStudy (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_EnvironmentAndEvidence> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EnvironmentAndEvidence" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EnvironmentAndEvidence .
pg:Interpreters a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Interpreters"@en ;
    skos:definition "The same program behaves differently under different Python releases, and PEP 594 explains some of it (Python Enhancement Proposals, 2026) (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Its leaves are PythonSeries, Pep594Modules, PyttsxReleases (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_Interpreters> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Interpreters" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EnvironmentAndEvidence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Interpreters .
pg:PythonSeries a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Python series"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Python 3.13 notes list the modules removed by PEP 594 and the PEP gives the reasons (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Python Enhancement Proposals, 2026). One program that saves speech, lists voices and asks yt-dlp questions ran under 11 interpreters (3.11, 3.12, 3.13, the seven 3.14 releases 3.14.0 to 3.14.6, and the beta 3.15.0b4) and compared 14 items: only 2 differed, both about the standard-library modules aifc and the others that Python 3.13 removed; the same program with Whisper compared 16 items and 2 differed, only in how a type is printed (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PythonSeries> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PythonSeries" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Interpreters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PythonSeries .
pg:Pep594Modules a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pep594 modules"@en ;
    skos:definition "PEP 594 removed aifc, audioop, chunk, sndhdr and sunau from the standard library in 3.13, and the 3.13 notes list them (Python Enhancement Proposals, 2026) (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Python 3.13 removed the modules aifc, audioop, chunk, sndhdr and sunau (PEP 594) and kept wave: on this interpreter {'aifc': False, 'audioop': False, 'sunau': False, 'chunk': False, 'sndhdr': False, 'wave': True}; under 3.11.17 and 3.12.3 all six imported (['3.11.17', '3.12.3']), under 3.13.14 and later none of the five did (3.13.14 to 3.15.0b4); the wave module, which pyttsx3 uses to write its file, is unaffected (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_Pep594Modules> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Pep594Modules" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Interpreters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Pep594Modules .
pg:PyttsxReleases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pyttsx releases"@en ;
    skos:definition "PyPI lists 22 releases of pyttsx3 (Python Package Index, 2026), and the project repository holds the drivers (Bhat, 2026). PyPI lists 22 releases of pyttsx3, the newest 2.99 of 2025-07-08; on Python 3.14.6 8 of them ran, 9 crashed with a segmentation fault (every release from 2.71 to 2.95 that could be installed) and 5 could not be installed because their pyobjc requirement needs macOS; the items that differ between the ones that ran are ['drivers in the package', 'module-level speak() exists', 'requires', 'save_to_file then runAndWait: wav', 'say(empty)', 'voices: count and first'], and releases before 2.97 listed 75 voices instead of 131, and the first five have no save_to_file at all (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_PyttsxReleases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PyttsxReleases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Interpreters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PyttsxReleases .
pg:Platforms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Platforms"@en ;
    skos:definition "pyttsx3 has one driver for each operating system: SAPI 5 on Windows, NSSS on macOS and eSpeak on Linux (Bhat, 2026) (Bhat, 2026) (Bhat, 2026). Its leaves are WindowsSapi5, MacNsss, LinuxEspeak (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_Platforms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Platforms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EnvironmentAndEvidence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Platforms .
pg:WindowsSapi5 a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Windows sapi5"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 SAPI 5 driver wraps the Microsoft Speech API, which Microsoft documents (Bhat, 2026) (Microsoft Learn, 2026). On Windows pyttsx3 uses the SAPI 5 driver sapi5.py; the study read its source (200) and Microsoft's page on SAPI 5.4 (200), but no Windows machine was available, so the driver, the voices David and Zira that the chapter lists and the speed of listing 4 were NOT EXECUTED (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_WindowsSapi5> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WindowsSapi5" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Platforms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WindowsSapi5 .
pg:MacNsss a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Mac nsss"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pyttsx3 NSSS driver wraps NSSpeechSynthesizer, which Apple documents (Bhat, 2026) (Apple Developer Documentation, 2026). On macOS pyttsx3 uses the NSSS driver nsss.py over NSSpeechSynthesizer; the study read its source (200) and Apple's page (200); the driver of the newest release also names an avspeech.py (True), but no Mac was available, so macOS output, its voices and the format of a saved file were NOT EXECUTED (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_MacNsss> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MacNsss" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Platforms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MacNsss .
pg:LinuxEspeak a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Linux espeak"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Ubuntu package archive lists espeak and espeak-ng, and the eSpeak NG README says it replaces eSpeak (Canonical, 2026) (eSpeak NG developers, 2026). On Ubuntu 24.04 the apt package espeak is version 1.48.15 and espeak-ng is 1.51; pyttsx3 2.99 loaded the library libespeak-ng.so.1 of the second, so the one-word difference in the chapter's command decides whether a program starts (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_LinuxEspeak> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LinuxEspeak" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Platforms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LinuxEspeak .
pg:Reproducibility a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reproducibility"@en ;
    skos:definition "A digest and a list of files let another reader check that the same text and files were used (Sweigart, 2025) (No Starch Press, 2025). Its leaves are ChapterDigest, OnlineMaterials (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_Reproducibility> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Reproducibility" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EnvironmentAndEvidence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Reproducibility .
pg:ChapterDigest a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chapter digest"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter page of the 3rd edition is the text the study read (Sweigart, 2025), and the table of contents and the book page place it in the book (Sweigart, 2025) (No Starch Press, 2025). The chapter page was fetched on 2026-10-14 and returned 200 with 44667 bytes; its SHA-256 239d91a73d23..."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_ChapterDigest> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChapterDigest" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reproducibility .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChapterDigest .
pg:OnlineMaterials a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Online materials"@en ;
    skos:definition "No Starch Press links the online materials of the book from its book page (No Starch Press, 2025) (No Starch Press, 2025). The online materials of the 3rd edition (7759877 bytes, 91 files, 72 of them Python) hold ['hello_tts.py'] as the only file of this chapter, and no file imports whisper or yt_dlp ([]); no listing of the chapter other than hello_tts.py can be taken from the materials (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_OnlineMaterials> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OnlineMaterials" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reproducibility .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OnlineMaterials .
pg:HonestyOfTheStudy a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Honesty of the study"@en ;
    skos:definition "The study labels what it did not execute and every single run. Its leaves are NotExecutedList, SingleRunLabels (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_HonestyOfTheStudy> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HonestyOfTheStudy" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:EnvironmentAndEvidence .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HonestyOfTheStudy .
pg:NotExecutedList a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Not executed list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The study names what it could not run, so that a reader does not take a reading of documentation for a result (Sweigart, 2025). The reasons for what could not be run are facts of the machine: /dev/snd exists False, nvidia-smi is installed False, torch.cuda.is_available() is False, the system is Linux and the memory is 8029 MiB; so nothing was heard, no GPU or large model was used, and Windows, macOS, cloud engines that need keys and downloads from YouTube were NOT EXECUTED (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_NotExecutedList> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NotExecutedList" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:HonestyOfTheStudy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NotExecutedList .
pg:SingleRunLabels a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Single run labels"@en ;
    skos:definition "Each record carries its date and the label of a single run. Each record of the study carries its date and the label of a single run: the models record (2026-10-14), the corpus record (2026-10-14), the interpreter record (2026-10-14) and the live record (2026-10-14) were all observed on 2026-10-14, and none of their timings was repeated, so a timing is one measurement on two cores (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch24#S_SingleRunLabels> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SingleRunLabels" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:HonestyOfTheStudy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SingleRunLabels .
